{
  "requirement": "`InspectionContext::read` returns the node's application-defined\n`D::NodeContent`, or the domain's read error.\n\nThe SDK's `read_node` tool accepts a known node's `NodePath` and returns its\ncontents formatted under\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.agent-calls.judge.rendering`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations**\n1. `InspectionContext::read` returns the node's `D::NodeContent`, or the domain's read error.\n2. The `read_node` tool takes a known node's `NodePath` and returns its contents formatted through `NodeContents::format`, under `judge.rendering`. That requirement is cited, so I only checked that its application is exercised here. It says that failures while loading or preparing a node's contents are returned to the agent as a tool error, so the agent can recover.\n\n**Coverage**\n- **Content from `read`:** `tests/validation.rs#fn[snapshots_record_exact_reads_and_retain_distinct_versions]` reads node 1 and asserts the returned content is `\"before\"`. It then changes the stored content and asserts a second read returns `\"after\"`.\n- **Read error from `read`:** the same test asserts that `inspection.read(&2)` is an error. `read` is declared as `Result<D::NodeContent, D::Error>`, so the type guarantees the error is the domain's own. The `is_err` assertion therefore exercises the failure branch, and I found no wrapped or substituted error that could slip past it.\n- **Formatted contents from the tool:** `agent/tests.rs#fn[concurrent_tool_agents_isolate_histories_pages_and_submissions_and_share_reads]` runs `read_node` with a formatter (`distinct_pages`) that produces `ALPHA-CONTENTS` or `BETA-CONTENTS` text. This text cannot come from the raw stored content `\"source\"`. The `ParallelConversations` provider asserts that each conversation's history contains its own formatted text. It also asserts that the other conversation's text is absent. This shows the tool returns the `format()` output for the requested path. The same test checks the recorded dependencies.\n- **Delivery and recording:** `fn[graph_tools_record_delivered_snapshots_and_prepared_pages_do_not_record_delivery]` calls `read_node` on node 2 with 18,000 characters of content. It asserts that the recorded `Dependency::Node` fingerprint matches the content and that pages are delivered. It also covers the failed-conversation cases, where nothing or only the delivered read is recorded.\n- **Preparation failure:** `fn[node_preparation_tool_errors_allow_recovery_without_recording_undelivered_contents]` makes the formatter fail. It asserts that the agent receives a tool result with `is_error` set, named `read_node`, carrying the error text. It then asserts that the agent can recover by submitting a result and that no dependency is recorded. This applies the `judge.rendering` tool-error rule.\n\n**Gaps**\nNone. The error-return clause for `read` is covered at the type level and by the node 2 test. The tool's success and failure paths each have an asserting test. The tool maps both read errors and formatting errors through the same preparation call, so the formatter-failure test covers the tool's error path.",
  "references": "b2022ad3523c8a5b18f5d424c8e474e87c57c0d273f0adc721b4dfa59187c682",
